**B4 · THE FIXED TEXT PARTS FOR THE TICKETS** (Tuesday's read-whole approves each, byte for byte; ASCII; each part is one backtick-free line after `=`).
- `<JSON-SUMMARY>` = JsonStorage's constructor probes its data directory with a fixed file name (.write-test): two simultaneous constructions can unlink each other's probe and one falls back to /tmp/printer-dashboard-data (builder's figure: 20 of 40 simultaneous pairs, 0 of 80 one after the other)
- `<JSON-BLUF>` = BLUF: the builder of RD-873 (2026-10-27) found that the JsonStorage constructor in backend/jsonStorage.js checks that its data directory is writable by writing a probe file with a fixed name, <dataDir>/.write-test, and then unlinking it (lines 1250 to 1253; a second probe at lines 832 to 834). If two processes construct a store on the same directory at the same moment, one can unlink the other's probe: the loser's unlink throws ENOENT, the constructor reads that as an unusable directory and points the store at /tmp/printer-dashboard-data. The builder measured it with its own script on one machine: 40 pairs of simultaneous constructions, 20 pairs with a child on the fallback, all 20 with the ENOENT warning; 40 pairs one after the other, 0 of 80 children on the fallback. The gate (B776) did not run the script and neither confirms nor refutes it. This ticket asks for the measurement to be repeated and, if it holds, for a small fix; nothing is built here.
- `<JSON-D1>` = Why it may matter (the builder's own claim, not verified by the gate or by this record): the same race would explain why the rd407 test children sometimes write the shared /tmp directory (the nondeterminism seen at the earlier B617 gate), and on a production path a Container Apps revision swap runs two processes on one share for up to 112 seconds, which could send a booting process to ephemeral scratch storage while the auth gate fails closed. Related tickets on the same family of fallbacks: RD-408 (an unwritable DATA_DIR falls back) and RD-407 (concurrent writers wipe settings.json), both Release Ready.
- `<JSON-D2>` = Fix shape (the builder's, not applied): give each process a unique probe name (or create it with the wx flag and a random suffix) and tolerate ENOENT on the unlink. Once that is done, the test double of CodeQL alert 97 (the nonatomic settings writer in __tests__/helpers/rd407-setting-writer.js) would never receive a path outside its directory, which reopens the question of how to bound that site (RD-873).
- `<JSON-D3>` = Not known: whether the race reproduces on the Linux runner or on the production share (the figures are one machine's, from the builder's script session-tools/b770/bin/race.js, which the gate did not run because it may write the shared /tmp/printer-dashboard-data that other runs read); whether any store was ever re-pointed to the fallback in production. Any repeat of the measurement must use its own temporary directory for the data directory and must not write /tmp/printer-dashboard-data.
- `<JSON-ACC>` = Done when: (1) the race is reproduced or refuted by a script that constructs the store in two processes against one directory, with its control (constructions one after the other read 0), recorded here with the commands; (2) if reproduced, a fix with a cell that is red against the fixed-name probe and green after it; this is a production file (backend/jsonStorage.js), so it is a build ticket with its own gate, tier chosen by the coordinator; (3) no change to the test files of RD-873 in this ticket. Related: RD-873, RD-408, RD-407.
- `<JSON-SRC>` = Source: builder B770 STATUS, FINDINGS 1 and its FOR TUESDAY ticket draft (Briefs/2026-10-27_B770_STATUS.md, lines 104 and 120); gate B776 STATUS, line 103 (race script NOT RUN); the coordinator's 27 Oct ruling that the next records seat files this ticket and builds nothing (B770 ANSWER-1); landing of PR #122: merge commit e24f16f5 (2026-10-27 05:38 AEDT). Board searched first: <SEARCHES> where <SEARCHES> is built by program from your own A3 searches as "JQL -> count (keys)" separated by " | ".
- `<FALSY-SUMMARY>` = PUT /api/risk-conditions/:id stores a falsy risk_level (0, false, null or an empty string) and answers 200, and a stored 0, false or null empties the Testing page's condition dropdown (static/js/testing.js line 41)
- `<FALSY-BLUF>` = BLUF: since PR #123 (RD-878, merge commit 6e58ddf7) a non-string risk_level such as 7 or ["high"] answers 400, but the guard in backend/server.js (one line, line 21233 at the gated head) is written updates.risk_level && ..., so the falsy values 0, false, null and the empty string skip it, answer 200 and are stored as sent (measured by the builder B777 and the gate B780; the answers are identical before and after the PR). A stored 0, false or null breaks the shipped Testing page: static/js/testing.js line 41 calls condition.risk_level.toUpperCase() with no guard, populateConditionDropdown() throws a TypeError on that row and renders no condition. No server reader answered 500. The state needs an administrator, or the open first-run window, and an API client; the shipped pages never send a falsy level. Pre-existing, not caused by PR #123.
- `<FALSY-D1>` = Reproduction (gate B780, driven in node with the file's own function, not in a browser): in the open first-run window, PUT /api/risk-conditions/1 with {"risk_level": null} answers 200; the Testing page's loader then fails on that row, the dropdown is empty and the page shows its risk-conditions error message (the message wording is read from code, not seen). The oracle: static/js/index.js and static/js/settings.js guard the same field with || ''.
- `<FALSY-D2>` = Fix shape, two separable parts (not applied): (a) the reader: guard static/js/testing.js line 41 the way index.js and settings.js do, with a cell that renders a condition whose stored level is null, 0 or false; (b) the writer: whether PUT should refuse a present but falsy risk_level with the same 400 (it would change the answer for the empty string and for null, which no shipped page sends). Part (b) is a scope question for the product owner (gate note B780-2), because RD-878's own text sets the falsy values aside; part (a) needs no answer.
- `<FALSY-D3>` = Counts: a new cell moves scripts/verify-expected-counts.json from 5170 tests in 307 suites; a row added inside an existing cell moves nothing. The gate also described, without writing it, a cell that sends a non-string level together with a valid condition_text and is red against a type test on the wrong field (note B780-3); it belongs with the writer change if one is made.
- `<FALSY-ACC>` = Done when: (1) the Testing page lists every condition even when one stored level is null, 0 or false, shown by a cell that is red against line 41 as it stands; (2) the product owner has answered the writer question (b) and, if yes, it is built and gated on its own; (3) a full npm run verify prints VERDICT: PASS and regenerates the counts file if a cell was added. Related: RD-878, {PUTVAL-KEY}, RD-836.
